> Ad platforms and marketplaces show bidders a bid landscape, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice to them or to their agents, which increasingly run on reasoning language models. We ask where the value of the reasoning tokens goes in that decision. Six small ($\le$14B) open models choose among 5 or 7 bids with empirical win curves from eBay prices, under forced budgets of up to 768 tokens, and are scored on held-out auctions against an exact calculator. They recover 42--85\% of the calculator's lead. Arithmetic is a large share of the loss for one model only; most of the remaining loss comes after the trace has already written the best option's value as the largest. Two failures produce it. The Qwen models run out of tokens before finishing the list of options, and the same trace, allowed to finish, usually answers correctly. Phi-mini and Granite-8B finish the list and then answer against it in 40--50\% of finished traces, following list position, a pattern that replicates on synthetic auctions. Wrong answers lean toward the bid most likely to win, which costs bidders under pay-your-bid pricing but not under eBay's own rule. Sending answers that disagree with the trace's own numbers to a calculator recovers 73--93\% of the gap on held-out products and 82--100\% on synthetic traces.
