> Forcing 768 tokens recovers 42--85\% of the calculator's lead (Table~\ref{tab:main}), almost all of it above 256 tokens; without reasoning the models are close to random. Recovery stays at 40--87\% when the choices are re-scored under three other win curves, including one where rivals bid the first-price equilibrium. The unforced run mostly measures more tokens, since most of its traces hit the 1{,}024 cap; Granite-8B is the exception and always stops on its own. The obvious reply to a 768-token cap, giving the model more tokens, has a price: at 768 tokens a decision already takes 9--35 seconds and 2--10\,kJ on one GPU, a lot for an agent that sets bids across many keywords or listings, and energy grows about linearly with the budget. The 1{,}024-token run shows what extra tokens buy: recovery rises to 72--96\% for five models, mostly by finishing the list, but stays at 49\% for Phi-mini (Table~\ref{tab:main}).
